          %meta{:content => "application/xhtml+xml;charset=utf-8", "http-equiv" => "Content-type"}/
          %title Bidirectional typechecking
          %h1#bidirectional_typechecking Bidirectional typechecking
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#for_dependent_types"} For dependent types
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#other_type_theories"} Other type theories
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Bidirectional typechecking
            is both a style of mathematical presentation of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            and a kind of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algorithm"} algorithm
            for implementing one. The idea is that the basic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t:A
            that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            belongs to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is split into two judgments:
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ⇐
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \Leftarrow A
              \: the term
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
              can be
              %em checked
              to have the type
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \Rightarrow A
              \: from the term
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
              we can
              = succeed "," do
                %em infer
              = succeed "," do
                %em synthesize
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              that it has.
          %p
            The difference is that in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ⇐
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\Leftarrow A
            we regard both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as “given” or “inputs”, whereas in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\Rightarrow A
            we regard only
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            as an “input” and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as an “output” that is “computed” from
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deductive+system"} deductive system
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi check
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} check(t,A)
              which simply succeeds or fails.
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi infer
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} infer(t)
              which either returns a type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              or fails.
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic+programming"} logic programming
          %p
            For example, from an un-annotated
            = succeed "-abstraction" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda x.x
            we cannot infer its type: the variable
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %em check
            that it has any given endomorphism type:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇐
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x.x) \Leftarrow (A\to A)
            for any type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\Rightarrow (A\to B)
            (for instance,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            could be a variable, since variables are declared with their types), and we can check that an argument
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            has type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            (that is,
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⇐
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\Leftarrow A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a)
            has type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a) \Rightarrow B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi M
                      %mo ⇐
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi λ
                      %mi x
                      %mo .
                      %mi M
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⇐
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi f
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⇐
                      %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi B
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma,x:A \vdash M \Leftarrow B}{\Gamma\vdash (\lambda x.M) \Leftarrow (A\to B)} \qquad \frac{\Gamma\vdash f \Rightarrow (A\to B) \qquad \Gamma\vdash a \Leftarrow A }{\Gamma \vdash f(a) \Rightarrow B}.
          %p
            Bidirectional typechecking is closely related to “canonical forms only” presentations of type theories (such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+frameworks"} logical frameworks
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                hereditary substitution
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/hereditary+substitution"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a)
            by inferring a type for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %em check
            the type of an un-annotated abstraction
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda x.M
            = succeed "-redex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/beta-reduction"} beta-reduction
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x.M)(a)
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                type ascription
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/type+ascription"} ?
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ⇐
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\Leftarrow A
            %em infer
            the same type of the
            %em ascribed
            term
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (t:A)\Rightarrow A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo .
                    %mi M
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\lambda x.M : A\to B)(a)
          .un_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              There are many different notations used in the literature for the bidirectional typing judgments. Sometimes one finds
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\rightrightarrows A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ⇇
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\leftleftarrows A
              in place of our
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\Rightarrow A
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ⇐
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\Leftarrow A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in A
              for inferring and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∋
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\ni t
              for checking. Sometimes upwards and downwards-pointing arrows are used instead of leftwards and rightwards ones (e.g.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \uparrow A
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \downarrow A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %msup
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t :^\uparrow A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %msup
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t :^\downarrow A
              or a dozen other variants) — but unfortunately there is no consensus on which judgment points up and which points down! It seems to be somewhat more common for checking to point up and inference to point down, as this matches the direction that “information flows through the derivation tree”, but the opposite convention is found in plenty of papers too. (It could be that there is a “modern” consensus that only older papers violate.)
